Third Session
Five Policy Questions

Question: The learning of emperors and kings must grasp its essentials. From antiquity, sage and wise sovereigns have without exception inquired into the Way with an open mind and set down rules of conduct close to their own persons. Whether in the admonitions inscribed on carriages, washbasins, doors, and windows, or in the chapters on "Receiving Instruction" and "Taking Counsel at the Beginning of the Reign" (Fangluo), such was the way their learning grasped the essentials. From the Three Dynasties onward, we need not discuss every case in detail. Our Sacred Ancestor [Emperor Taizu] founded the dynasty and elucidated the transmission of the Way; for instance, he personally explicated the Book of Documents (Shangshu) and the Analects (Lunyu), and discussed such works as the Book of Changes (Zhouyi), the Great Learning (Daxue), and the Mencius (Mengzi), truly attaining the secret of the mind-to-mind transmission of a thousand ages. The Successive Sacred Emperors carried on the heritage, their minds connected at the source. Thus there were the Essentials of the Mind in Imperial Learning (Shengxue xinfa), the Direct Pointers to the Classics (Zhujing zhizhi), the compilation of the Mirror for Rulers of Successive Dynasties (Lidai junjian), and the composition and transmission of the Admonition on Reverent Unity (Jingyi) and the Five Admonitions (Wuzhen). Do all of these indeed tally with those of the ancient emperors and kings or not? Now…
